<script>
  import Aside from '$lib/components/Aside.svelte';
  import Screenshot from '$lib/components/Screenshot.svelte';
</script>

In the summer of 2024, reporters at Gothamist pulled the NYPD's public database of shooting incidents and noticed something striking. The same blocks turned up again and again, year after year.

They built an interactive map to show what they found.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/gothamist-gun-violence.png"
  alt="Gothamist's 'Map: Where New York City's shootings happen' showing a map of the five boroughs shaded by census block, with the darkest blocks concentrated in parts of the Bronx, Brooklyn, and Harlem"
  chromeTitle="Map: Where New York City's shootings happen"
  chromeUrl="https://gothamist.com/news/new-york-city-gun-violence-map"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

The map shades every census block in the five boroughs by how many shooting incidents the NYPD recorded there since 2020. The pattern it reveals — gun violence concentrating on a small number of blocks, mostly surrounding NYCHA developments — became the spine of a companion investigation.

One thing to notice in the Gothamist map is what it does *not* show. There are no individual dots. Every incident is aggregated to its census block, so the reader sees the pattern without being pointed at a specific address. That was a choice. Individual points would have been more dramatic, but they would also have stigmatized specific doorsteps and sidewalks where real people live. The editors traded some visual punch for more responsible journalism.

Today you are going to build an interactive map with the same techniques Gothamist used — a base map, filtered points, popups, a choropleth view — but about a different New York City story. One that has nothing to do with gun violence and everything to do with what you walk past every day on the way to class.

## Part 1: The most common tree in New York

Take a walk down almost any street in Manhattan and look up. Chances are, the canopy above you belongs to a [London planetree](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Platanus_%C3%97_acerifolia).

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/london-plane-central-park.jpg"
  alt="A row of mature London planetrees with distinctive mottled, peeling bark lining a path in Central Park"
  chromeTitle=""
  chromeUrl=""
  caption="A London planetree in Central Park. Its patchwork bark is the giveaway."
  maxWidth="720px"
/>

The London planetree is a hybrid between the American sycamore and the Oriental plane tree. It is tough, tolerant of pollution and compacted soil, and indifferent to heavy pruning. It can survive in conditions that would kill most other large shade trees.

One man loved it above all others.

Robert Moses, the unelected power broker who ran New York's Department of Parks and Recreation from 1934 to 1960, made the London planetree his signature tree. He planted them along his parkways. He planted them in his parks. He planted them on street after street of every borough he rebuilt. The leaf in the NYC Parks department's green-and-white logo — the one on every trash can and park sign in the city — is widely believed to be a London planetree leaf. When Moses started an agency newsletter he called it *The Sycamore*, another name for the same tree.

The legacy of Moses is usually told through his bridges, his expressways, and the neighborhoods his bulldozers erased. It can also be told through his trees. Today New York has something like 660,000 street trees, and roughly one in ten of them is a London planetree. That means that whenever you see one, you are often looking at a direct artifact of one man's taste imposed on the city a lifetime ago.

<Aside title="A tree planted for the wrong reasons?">

<p>
    In March 2024, Slate published
    <a href="https://slate.com/technology/2024/03/london-planetrees-new-york-parks-nonnative-species-bad-why.html" target="_blank">an argument</a>
    that London planetrees shouldn't be New York's most common street tree. The case:
    a non-native hybrid planted in monoculture suppresses local biodiversity. Birds
    and insects that evolved alongside native oaks and maples get much less out of
    Moses's chosen tree. The reporter called the London planetree "a triumph of
    engineering and a failure of imagination."
</p>

<p>
    More recently, arborists have noticed London planetrees shedding unusual amounts
    of bark during heat waves, raising questions about whether the tree that survived
    the Industrial Revolution is finally being overwhelmed by the climate crisis.
</p>

</Aside>

That's the story behind our data.

In 2015, NYC Parks ran its third decadal street tree census — a citizen science effort called [TreesCount!](https://www.nycgovparks.org/trees/treescount) — and mapped every single street tree in the city. Species, trunk diameter, health status, exact location. Over 2,200 volunteers walked every block in New York with tablets, and they found 666,134 trees.

We are going to pull the London planetrees out of that census — roughly 70,000 of them — and build an interactive map that lets readers find every Moses tree in their neighborhood, inspect its health, and see how Moses's planting pattern still shapes the canopy of the five boroughs today.

### Mapping responsibly

Before we write a line of code, a brief note on ethics.

The Gothamist team aggregated their data to census blocks because pinpointing individual shootings would have done harm. Our data has no such sensitivity — trees are not people, and nothing about this project can hurt a human being. But the same instinct applies in a gentler form. When we build a map, we are making choices about what the reader sees, what they miss, and where their attention goes. Plotting individual points tells one story. Aggregating to neighborhoods tells another. Both are true; neither is complete.

We'll build both views today, and you'll see for yourself how the same data can produce wildly different impressions.

### Set up your project

Open your browser and navigate to our template repository at <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/template-repo-homepage.png"
  alt="GitHub template repository page showing the Use this template button"
  chromeTitle="cuny-jour-static-site-template"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template"
/>

Click "Use this template" and create a new repository called `moses-trees`. Clone it, open it in Visual Studio Code, and install the dependencies you know so well:

```bash
npm install
```

Then install one more package we need this week — [MapLibre GL JS](https://maplibre.org/), the open-source mapping library that powers our map:

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl
```

This is the first time we've added a dependency beyond what the template ships with. Before, `npm install` was pulling down whatever the template's `package.json` declared. Now we are asking npm to add something new to that list. If you open `package.json` you should see `maplibre-gl` there now. When a classmate clones your repository later and runs `npm install`, they'll get MapLibre automatically.

Now grab the data. I've posted two files in the class chat: `london-planetrees.geojson` and `community-districts.geojson`. Download them and drop both into your project's `static/` folder.

Start the development server:

```bash
npm run dev
```

And open [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173) in your browser.

## Part 2: Your first map

The class template already ships with the two components we need to build our map: `Map` and `MapLayer`. You'll find them in `src/lib/components/`. Open `Map.svelte` and read the comment block at the top. It explains what the component does and how to use it.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/vscode-map-component.png"
  alt="VS Code showing the Map.svelte component's opening comment block with its USAGE EXAMPLE"
  showChrome={false}
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

`Map` is a wrapper around MapLibre. It handles the fiddly parts — creating the map, loading the basemap tiles, re-initializing on theme changes, cleaning up on unmount — so you don't have to. All you need to give it is a center, a zoom level, and an optional theme. It will render whatever you put inside it as a layer on top of the base map.

Let's see it in action.

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` and replace the contents with:

```svelte
<script>
  import Map from '$lib/components/Map.svelte';
</script>

<h1>Moses trees</h1>

<Map
  longitude={-73.968}
  latitude={40.785}
  zoom={12}
  theme="positron"
  caption="A quiet map of Manhattan, waiting for data."
  credit="OpenFreeMap / OpenStreetMap contributors"
/>
```

Save the file and check your browser. You should see a clean pale-gray map of Manhattan with a caption underneath.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/bare-map.png"
  alt="A pale-gray map of Manhattan with no data overlaid, sitting below an H1 reading 'Moses trees'"
  chromeTitle="Moses trees"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Play with it a little. Drag the map around. Zoom in and out with the scroll wheel. Then try changing the `theme` prop to `"liberty"` or `"bright"` and save — the basemap should swap without you reloading the page.

Change `longitude`, `latitude`, and `zoom` and watch the map fly to the new location. These aren't just initial values; they are live, reactive props. Remember everything you already know about `$state` and reactive props from weeks past. That knowledge now controls a map.

## Part 3: Finding your neighborhood

A map that starts centered on Midtown is nice, but a reader doesn't live in Midtown. They live in Bed-Stuy, or Flushing, or Mott Haven, or upstate somewhere and they're homesick. Let's give them a way to point the map at the place they care about.

The template includes a third component called `Geocoder`. It's a search box that translates a typed address into geographic coordinates using the free [Nominatim](https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/) service, the open-source alternative to Google Places. When the user picks a result, it fires an `onresult` callback with the coordinates.

Let's wire it up.

In `+page.svelte`, add the import and two reactive state variables for the coordinates and zoom:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="3,6-8"}
<script>
  import Map from '$lib/components/Map.svelte';
  import Geocoder from '$lib/components/Geocoder.svelte';

  // Start centered on the middle of Manhattan.
  let longitude = $state(-73.968);
  let latitude = $state(40.785);
  let zoom = $state(12);
</script>
```

Now replace the hard-coded numbers in the `<Map>` tag with the state variables, and add a `<Geocoder>` above the map with a callback that updates them.

```svelte {emphasize-lines="3-10,14-16"}
<h1>Moses trees</h1>

<Geocoder
  label="Find your neighborhood"
  placeholder="Enter an address in New York…"
  onresult={(result) => {
    longitude = result.lng;
    latitude = result.lat;
    zoom = 15;
  }}
/>

<Map
  {longitude}
  {latitude}
  {zoom}
  theme="positron"
  caption="A quiet map of Manhattan, waiting for data."
/>
```

Notice the shorthand `{longitude}` — that's the same as writing `longitude={longitude}`. Svelte lets you skip the repetition when the prop name matches the variable name. We've been using this pattern since week three.

Save and try it. Type your home address into the search box, pick a result from the dropdown, and watch the map glide over to your block. The animated transition is built into MapLibre — any time those props change, the underlying map calls `flyTo` with a smooth easing curve.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/geocoder-in-action.png"
  alt="The page showing a search box with the query 'Newmark Graduate School of Journalism', a dropdown of matching results, and a map that has flown to Midtown Manhattan"
  chromeTitle="Moses trees"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

This is the reactive pattern you know, now connected to cartography. The Geocoder updates three state variables. Those variables flow into the Map as props. The Map reacts to the change by animating to the new location. No imperative code, no manual event wiring. You describe what should be true, and Svelte and MapLibre work out how to get there.

Try searching for a few addresses outside Manhattan. Search for your childhood home. Search for a place you've always been curious about. This kind of personal, place-based interaction is one of the most engaging tools a journalist has. The reader enters the story through their own front door.

## Part 4: Loading the data

So far our map has no data. Let's fix that.

This is the first data-loaded page we've built this week, and we're going to use a pattern that is now familiar to you: the [`+page.js`](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/routing#page-page-js) load function we introduced in [week five](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-5). That file runs before the page renders, fetches whatever data the page needs, and hands it to `+page.svelte` as a prop. Every dataset-driven page we've built since has followed this shape, and this one is no different.

Create a new file at `src/routes/+page.js`:

```js
export async function load({ fetch }) {
  const trees = await fetch('/london-planetrees.geojson').then((r) => r.json());
  const districts = await fetch('/community-districts.geojson').then((r) => r.json());
  return { trees, districts };
}
```

The `fetch` function passed in by SvelteKit is a smarter version of the browser's built-in `fetch` — it works at build time as well as at runtime, so your static site gets the data baked in.

Now update the `<script>` block in `+page.svelte` to receive the loaded data:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="5-7"}
<script>
  import Map from '$lib/components/Map.svelte';
  import Geocoder from '$lib/components/Geocoder.svelte';

  let { data } = $props();
  const trees = data.trees;
  const districts = data.districts;

  let longitude = $state(-73.968);
  let latitude = $state(40.785);
  let zoom = $state(12);
</script>
```

Before we plot anything, let's confirm the data is really there. Add a `console.log` at the top of the script block:

```js
console.log('trees:', trees.features.length, 'records');
console.log('districts:', districts.features.length, 'polygons');
```

Save, open your browser's developer console (Cmd+Option+J on Mac, Ctrl+Shift+J on Windows), and refresh. You should see something like:

```
trees: 71248 records
districts: 71 polygons
```

(Your exact number of trees may vary slightly — the census is a snapshot.)

Seventy thousand trees. That's what we're about to put on the map.

## Part 5: Plotting the trees

Here's where the course's last few weeks pay off. You've seen `Map` as a component that can accept children. You've seen components that accept data props. You're about to compose the two.

The template's `MapLayer` component takes a GeoJSON collection and a MapLibre paint object and renders the data on the parent map. Place it as a child of `<Map>`:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2,10-19"}
<script>
  import MapLayer from '$lib/components/MapLayer.svelte';
  // ...existing imports and state...
</script>

<!-- Geocoder -->

<Map {longitude} {latitude} {zoom} theme="positron" caption="Every London planetree in New York City, according to the 2015 Street Tree Census.">
  <MapLayer
    id="planetrees"
    type="circle"
    data={trees}
    paint={{
      'circle-color': '#2d6a4f',
      'circle-radius': 3,
      'circle-opacity': 0.6
    }}
  />
</Map>
```

Save. Your map should now be covered in green dots.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/first-dots.png"
  alt="A pale-gray map of Manhattan covered in small green dots representing London planetrees, densest along Riverside Drive, the Avenue of the Americas, and Central Park West"
  chromeTitle="Moses trees"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Zoom out to see the whole city. The dots stretch from the tip of Staten Island to the top of the Bronx. You can see Moses's planting corridors — dense lines along parkways, thick clusters around the parks he built. This is the story of New York's midcentury green infrastructure, rendered in a single dataset.

But there's a problem. When you zoom out, the individual dots collapse into one inky blur. When you zoom in, they look tiny. A good map should work at every zoom level.

### Zoom-dependent styling

MapLibre lets you tie paint properties to the current zoom level using [interpolate expressions](https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/expressions/#interpolate). They look odd at first, but the idea is simple: at zoom 10, use this value; at zoom 15, use that value; smoothly blend in between.

Update your `MapLayer`:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="6-17"}
<MapLayer
  id="planetrees"
  type="circle"
  data={trees}
  paint={{
    'circle-color': '#2d6a4f',
    'circle-radius': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
      10, 1.5,
      14, 4,
      18, 8
    ],
    'circle-opacity': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
      10, 0.4,
      14, 0.8
    ]
  }}
/>
```

Reading the first expression out loud: *interpolate linearly by the current zoom level. At zoom 10, use radius 1.5. At zoom 14, use 4. At zoom 18, use 8.* MapLibre smooths between the stops automatically.

Save and zoom around. At city-wide scale, the dots are small and translucent — perfect for seeing the density pattern. At neighborhood scale, they grow bold enough to click. This is also a performance trick: MapLibre's GPU renders small, translucent circles very cheaply, so 70,000 of them don't bog down the map.

Take a minute and really explore the map. Find your street. Find your school. Find the block where your favorite bodega sits. If you grew up in New York, find your childhood home.

Notice, too, what the map is *not* showing. It's not showing the oaks, the maples, the honeylocusts, or the 160-odd other species the census caught. Just Moses's tree. The negative space — the streets without a green dot on them — is as much a part of this story as the dense ribbons of dots along the old parkways.

## Part 6: Adding popups

Readers always want to click things. Let's reward that instinct.

`MapLayer` accepts a `popup` prop: a function that takes a clicked feature and returns an HTML string. MapLayer handles the rest — positioning the popup, wiring the click handler, changing the cursor on hover.

Add a `popup` function to your `MapLayer`:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="19-27"}
<MapLayer
  id="planetrees"
  type="circle"
  data={trees}
  paint={{
    'circle-color': '#2d6a4f',
    'circle-radius': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
      10, 1.5,
      14, 4,
      18, 8
    ],
    'circle-opacity': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
      10, 0.4,
      14, 0.8
    ]
  }}
  popup={(feature) => {
    const p = feature.properties;
    return `
      <strong>${p.address || 'London planetree'}</strong><br>
      Status: ${p.status || 'Unknown'}<br>
      Health: ${p.health || 'Not recorded'}<br>
      Trunk: ${p.tree_dbh ? p.tree_dbh + ' inches across' : 'Unknown'}
    `;
  }}
/>
```

Save. Zoom in on the map, then click one of the green dots. A popup should appear with the tree's address, status, health, and trunk diameter.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/popup.png"
  alt="A zoomed-in map view showing a cluster of green dots with a popup open over one, listing the tree's address, status as Alive, health as Good, and trunk diameter of 24 inches"
  chromeTitle="Moses trees"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

A few things worth noticing about the `popup` prop.

It's a *function*, not a string. That's the key to making popups flexible. You pass in a template function, and MapLayer calls it every time a tree is clicked. Because the function has access to the clicked feature's full properties object, you can construct whatever HTML makes sense for the data at hand.

It returns ordinary HTML as a string. That's why we use backtick [template literals](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Template_literals) here — they let us interpolate variables right into the markup with the `${}` syntax. You could put any inline styling or additional markup in here that you want.

And it gracefully handles missing data. Some trees in the census lack an address, some lack a health rating, some are stumps without a trunk diameter. The `||` fallbacks mean the popup never displays literal `undefined`. This kind of defensive formatting separates professional work from a prototype.

## Part 7: Filtering the map

Seventy thousand is too many to absorb at once. A good interactive map gives the reader tools to slice the data and find the story inside it.

Our tree records have two fields that make great filters: `health` (a rating from Good to Poor, or nothing for dead or stump trees) and `tree_dbh` (trunk diameter in inches, which is a proxy for age).

Let's add controls for both.

### The health filter

In `+page.svelte`, add a state variable for the selected health rating:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="5"}
let longitude = $state(-73.968);
let latitude = $state(40.785);
let zoom = $state(12);

let healthFilter = $state('all');
```

Now create a `$derived` filtered version of the trees. It will be recalculated every time a filter changes, and passed to `MapLayer` instead of the raw data.

```js
let filteredTrees = $derived.by(() => {
  const features = trees.features.filter((f) => {
    if (healthFilter !== 'all' && f.properties.health !== healthFilter) return false;
    return true;
  });
  return { type: 'FeatureCollection', features };
});
```

Note the `$derived.by(() => {...})` form. When you need a derived value that comes from a multi-step computation rather than a one-line expression, wrap the function body in `$derived.by`. The plain `$derived(expression)` form only works for a single expression.

Swap the data prop on `MapLayer` to use the filtered set. Change the one line on your existing `<MapLayer>`:

```svelte
  data={trees}
```

To read:

```svelte
  data={filteredTrees}
```

Leave every other prop on `MapLayer` alone.

And add a dropdown above the map:

```svelte
<label class="filter">
  Health:
  <select bind:value={healthFilter}>
    <option value="all">All</option>
    <option value="Good">Good</option>
    <option value="Fair">Fair</option>
    <option value="Poor">Poor</option>
  </select>
</label>
```

Save and try it. Pick "Poor" from the dropdown and watch the map collapse to a sparse scattering of sick trees. Pick "Good" and it fills back in.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/health-filter.png"
  alt="The map showing only London planetrees rated as Poor health, with far fewer dots visible, concentrated in parts of Queens and the Bronx"
  chromeTitle="Moses trees"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

This is the same reactive pattern you've used all semester — `$state` tracks the input, `$derived.by` recomputes, the UI updates. The only new part is that instead of filtering a list of HTML cards, we're filtering a layer on a map.

### The diameter filter

Trunk diameter is a rough proxy for age. A London planetree with a trunk over 30 inches across was very likely planted during Moses's tenure, seventy or eighty years ago. A trunk under 10 inches is a younger tree, planted by a later administration. Slicing on diameter lets the reader separate the old guard from the new growth.

Add a second state variable and a slider that controls the minimum diameter:

```svelte {emphasize-lines="2,12-17"}
let healthFilter = $state('all');
let minDiameter = $state(0);
```

Extend the filter to respect it:

```js {emphasize-lines="4"}
let filteredTrees = $derived.by(() => {
  const features = trees.features.filter((f) => {
    if (healthFilter !== 'all' && f.properties.health !== healthFilter) return false;
    if (f.properties.tree_dbh < minDiameter) return false;
    return true;
  });
  return { type: 'FeatureCollection', features };
});
```

Add the slider control to the markup:

```svelte
<label class="filter">
  Minimum trunk size: {minDiameter} inches
  <input type="range" bind:value={minDiameter} min="0" max="60" step="1">
</label>
```

Save and drag the slider to 30. Most of the dots disappear. What remains is the living ghost of Moses's mid-century planting campaign — the trees he personally ordered into the ground, still shading the streets they were planted on.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/big-trees-only.png"
  alt="The map showing only London planetrees with trunk diameters of 30 inches or more, revealing Moses's original planting corridors along the parkways and the edges of his parks"
  chromeTitle="Moses trees"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That's a good map. That's journalism.

## Part 8: Showing the count

Let the reader see how many trees they're looking at. Add a derived count:

```js
let count = $derived(filteredTrees.features.length);
```

And print it above the map:

```svelte
<p class="count">
  {count.toLocaleString()} of {trees.features.length.toLocaleString()} London planetrees match your filters
</p>
```

The [`toLocaleString()`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Number/toLocaleString) method adds the commas — `71248` becomes `71,248`. A small detail that separates a working page from a polished one.

Toggle your filters and watch the number update instantly.

## Part 9: Editorial framing

The map works, but it doesn't yet *say* anything. Every good piece of interactive journalism tells the reader what they're looking at and why it matters. Let's add a proper framing.

Replace the bare `<h1>Moses trees</h1>` with a fuller header:

```svelte
<h1>Moses trees</h1>

<p class="dek">
  Robert Moses planted the London planetree along every parkway and
  in every park he built. Seven decades later, his favorite tree is
  still the most common street tree in New York. Search your
  neighborhood and see how many Moses left you.
</p>
```

And at the bottom, add a source credit and methodology note:

```svelte
<footer class="source">
  <p>
    Source: NYC Parks
    <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Environment/2015-Street-Tree-Census-Tree-Data/uvpi-gqnh" target="_blank">2015 Street Tree Census</a>,
    via NYC Open Data. Address geocoding by
    <a href="https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/" target="_blank">Nominatim</a>.
    Basemap tiles by <a href="https://openfreemap.org/" target="_blank">OpenFreeMap</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 2015 census is the most recent complete inventory of New York's
    street trees. Some trees have been removed or replaced since,
    and new ones have been planted. This map is a snapshot of what
    volunteers mapped a decade ago, not a live registry.
  </p>
</footer>
```

That second paragraph is the data caveat. It tells the reader the limitations of what they're looking at. This kind of honesty is what separates data journalism from data decoration. Never publish a map without it.

## Part 10: A choropleth view

The dot map is powerful, but it is only one way to read this data. A reader looking at 70,000 individual points might miss the broader geography — which *neighborhoods* carry the most Moses trees? Which districts have been short-changed?

A [choropleth map](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choropleth_map) answers that question directly. It divides the city into regions — in our case, the 59 community districts — and shades each region by a count or rate. It is the same technique Gothamist used at the start of the lesson. Only the subject and the granularity differ.

### Aggregating the data

Our community district GeoJSON has the polygons but no tree counts. We need to compute them in JavaScript, respecting whatever filters the reader has active.

Add another `$derived.by` that copies the districts GeoJSON and attaches a count to each district:

```js
let districtData = $derived.by(() => {
  // Count filtered trees per community district.
  const counts = {};
  for (const feature of filteredTrees.features) {
    const cb = feature.properties.cb_num;
    if (!cb) continue;
    counts[cb] = (counts[cb] || 0) + 1;
  }

  // Deep-copy the district polygons and attach the counts.
  const enriched = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(districts));
  for (const feature of enriched.features) {
    const cb = feature.properties.boro_cd;
    feature.properties.count = counts[cb] || 0;
  }
  return enriched;
});
```

Two things are worth a pause here. The `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(...))` is a quick-and-dirty way to deep-copy a JavaScript object. It's not fast, but for 59 district polygons it's plenty fast enough, and it means our mutations don't corrupt the original `districts` data. And the join key on the tree side (`cb_num`) matches the join key on the district side (`boro_cd`) — both are formatted as a three-digit BoroCD like `101` for Manhattan Community District 1, `303` for Brooklyn Community District 3, and so on. If you ever see zero counts everywhere, that's your signal that the keys don't match and you need to console-log both sides to see what shape they actually take.

### Adding a second MapLayer

Because the prefab `Map` can accept any number of children, we can simply add a second `MapLayer` underneath the first. But we want them to be toggleable, not both visible at once. Add a state variable for the view mode:

```js
let viewMode = $state('dots');
```

Add a toggle control above the map:

```svelte
<div class="view-toggle">
  <button
    class:active={viewMode === 'dots'}
    onclick={() => viewMode = 'dots'}
  >Individual trees</button>
  <button
    class:active={viewMode === 'choropleth'}
    onclick={() => viewMode = 'choropleth'}
  >By community district</button>
</div>
```

And swap the `MapLayer` with an `{#if}` block:

```svelte
<Map {longitude} {latitude} {zoom} theme="positron">
  {#if viewMode === 'dots'}
    <MapLayer
      id="planetrees"
      type="circle"
      data={filteredTrees}
      paint={{
        'circle-color': '#2d6a4f',
        'circle-radius': [
          'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
          10, 1.5, 14, 4, 18, 8
        ],
        'circle-opacity': [
          'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
          10, 0.4, 14, 0.8
        ]
      }}
      popup={(feature) => {
        const p = feature.properties;
        return `
          <strong>${p.address || 'London planetree'}</strong><br>
          Status: ${p.status || 'Unknown'}<br>
          Health: ${p.health || 'Not recorded'}<br>
          Trunk: ${p.tree_dbh ? p.tree_dbh + ' inches across' : 'Unknown'}
        `;
      }}
    />
  {:else}
    <MapLayer
      id="districts-fill"
      type="fill"
      data={districtData}
      paint={{
        'fill-color': [
          'interpolate', ['linear'], ['get', 'count'],
          0, '#f0f7f4',
          500, '#95d5b2',
          2000, '#40916c',
          5000, '#1b4332'
        ],
        'fill-opacity': 0.8
      }}
      popup={(feature) => {
        const p = feature.properties;
        return `
          <strong>Community District ${p.boro_cd}</strong><br>
          ${p.count.toLocaleString()} London planetrees
        `;
      }}
    />
    <MapLayer
      id="districts-outline"
      type="line"
      data={districtData}
      paint={{
        'line-color': '#1b4332',
        'line-width': 0.5
      }}
    />
  {/if}
</Map>
```

Save and click the "By community district" toggle. The individual dots disappear and the city fills with shaded polygons. The darker a district, the more Moses trees it holds. Click any district and a popup shows its exact count.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-11/choropleth.png"
  alt="A choropleth map of NYC's 59 community districts shaded in green, with darker shades concentrated in the Upper East Side, Upper West Side, and Park Slope, indicating the highest concentrations of London planetrees"
  chromeTitle="Moses trees"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Flip back and forth between the two views. Each tells a different story. The dots show you Moses's exact corridors — you can read the shape of his old parkways from the map. The choropleth shows you the bigger picture — which districts got the canopy, and which got left behind. Neither view is more true than the other. Offering both lets the reader choose the lens that answers their question.

The filters still work. Drag the diameter slider while viewing the choropleth and watch the counts shift — the districts that retain the most original Moses trees are not always the same as the districts with the most London planetrees overall.

## Part 11: Going further with AI

What we built today is a foundation. There is a lot more you could do with it, and this is a good moment to put your AI assistant to work.

Try asking Copilot to:

- Color the dots by health status: green for Good, yellow for Fair, red for Poor.
- Add a second filter for borough, as a dropdown.
- Add a color legend to the choropleth explaining what the shades mean.
- Add a third view mode that clusters dots into groups that expand when you zoom in.
- Replace the dropdown and slider with a single search that lets readers type a cross-street, snap the map to it, and list the five nearest Moses trees below.

MapLibre is a big library and its documentation is dense. This is exactly the kind of situation where a coding assistant shines. You describe the editorial intent; it translates that into the right API calls. Just remember to test each change and read the resulting code — if your assistant reaches for a pattern you don't understand, ask it to explain before you commit.

## Homework

### Task 1: Build your own interactive map

Find a dataset on the [NYC Open Data portal](https://opendata.cityofnewyork.us/) or another public source that includes latitude and longitude. Build an interactive map for it.

Your project must have:

- A `Map` component with real data plotted from your chosen dataset
- At least one `MapLayer` with a `popup` template
- At least one filter or interactive control that uses `$state` and `$derived.by`
- A `Geocoder` to let readers navigate to their own neighborhood
- An editorial frame: a headline, an introductory paragraph, and a source credit with a methodology caveat

Some datasets that could work well:

- NYC Film Permits (the industry's footprint on your block)
- Community Gardens (where the city grows food)
- FDNY Response Times (emergency service equity)
- 311 Noise Complaints (what New York complains about)
- Street Trees, a different species (honeylocust? callery pear?)
- Restaurant Inspections (letter grades by neighborhood)
- Motor Vehicle Crashes (Vision Zero)

Deploy to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to walk the class through your map and explain:

- The dataset you chose and what story the geography tells
- How you connected Svelte's reactive state to MapLibre through the `MapLayer` component
- At least one editorial choice you made about what to show and what to leave out — and why
- Anything you got from an AI assistant that you had to modify or rewrite yourself

### Task 3: Look at it on your phone

Before you present, open your live site on your phone. Does the map fill the screen? Do the filter controls stack cleanly? Can you tap a dot with your thumb?

Most readers of most interactive news maps will encounter them on a phone. A map that only works on a laptop is a map that fails most of its readers. Fix anything that isn't working before next class.
